1
00:00:00,589 --> 00:00:04,526
Proving a new form of family — not limited by blood.

2
00:00:04,585 --> 00:00:07,747
Family has traditionally been defined by blood.

3
00:00:07,797 --> 00:00:11,930
But trusted neighbors and friends can be family too.

4
00:00:11,984 --> 00:00:15,147
And here, an AI joins as an agent.

5
00:00:16,074 --> 00:00:18,511
Let's walk through a concrete example.

6
00:00:18,651 --> 00:00:30,115
Say we need to pay ¥3M in hospital fees, and we ask Claude to handle it.
Claude judges it a high-value transfer and sets Tier 2 — two approvals required.

7
00:00:31,316 --> 00:00:41,443
Can Claude approve this on its own? The answer is no.
An AI holds no secret, so it can't create a ZK proof.

8
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:49,400
Now, Neighbor A approves this proposal.

9
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:59,000
But that's only the first approval.
The threshold isn't reached, so it doesn't execute yet.

10
00:01:02,167 --> 00:01:04,643
Then Neighbor B approves the same proposal —

11
00:01:04,693 --> 00:01:12,242
With the second approval the threshold is met,
and only then is it executed.

12
00:01:16,687 --> 00:01:19,813
On the other hand, for a low-risk action —

13
00:01:19,992 --> 00:01:26,583
Asked to "send a schedule reminder", Claude judges the impact small and picks Tier 0.

14
00:01:26,964 --> 00:01:30,228
No approval needed — the AI can act on its own.

15
00:01:34,683 --> 00:01:37,893
So how is this trust actually proven?

16
00:01:37,992 --> 00:01:41,398
Proving you're family without revealing the secret.

17
00:01:41,885 --> 00:01:47,471
Without revealing the secret at all,
a ZK proof shows you are a member of the family.

18
00:01:47,957 --> 00:01:51,331
On the testnet, verification succeeds.

19
00:01:55,803 --> 00:01:58,536
But what if the secret is stolen?

20
00:01:58,805 --> 00:02:06,264
With the same secret, a second proof is sent for a different challenge.
On-chain, this one passes too.

21
00:02:10,838 --> 00:02:17,587
But the notifier compares the two proofs
and mathematically recovers the secret itself.

22
00:02:17,812 --> 00:02:21,000
A stolen secret works against the attacker the more it is used.

23
00:02:26,168 --> 00:02:29,086
During the event, we built protection against impersonation.

24
00:02:29,165 --> 00:02:35,788
A call between Mom and her "daughter". The voice sounds identical —
but it could be an AI voice clone.

25
00:02:36,403 --> 00:02:42,293
Mom picks a passphrase on the spot,
and the daughter binds it into her World ID proof.

26
00:02:43,595 --> 00:02:45,724
The daughter approves it on her phone.

27
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:55,300
An Orb-verified real human. A matching passphrase. A registered family member.

28
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:59,100
With all three, the call is accepted.

29
00:02:59,648 --> 00:03:03,935
Next: the passphrase is right, but it's not family.

30
00:03:03,991 --> 00:03:09,685
Even if a scammer who overheard the passphrase proves with their own World ID…

31
00:03:09,925 --> 00:03:13,361
they are not a registered family member, so it is rejected.

32
00:03:21,598 --> 00:03:24,171
Family isn't an attribute — it's a relationship.

33
00:03:24,221 --> 00:03:32,177
AI and trusted people working as family, while protecting privacy.
That's the future we're building.

